The channel name is not a security boundary—any loaded page or injected script can discover and call `SuperSecretBridge`. I wouldn’t let it directly unlock premium.

Use the bridge only to submit a bounded, typed completion signal, then have Flutter verify the purchase with the entitlement-owning backend:

```dart
controller.addJavaScriptChannel(
  'SuperSecretBridge',
  onMessageReceived: (message) async {
    final event = CheckoutEvent.tryParse(message.message);
    if (event == null ||
        event.action != CheckoutAction.purchaseCompleted ||
        !trustedCheckoutPageIsCurrent() ||
        !consumeOnce(event.correlationId)) {
      return;
    }

    // Server validates payment and returns authoritative entitlement state.
    final entitlement = await entitlementRepository.refreshFromServer();
    if (!mounted || entitlement.accountId != currentAccountId) return;

    entitlementController.replaceWith(entitlement);
  },
);
```

The message should contain only values such as a version, action, and one-time correlation ID—never an asserted entitlement:

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "action": "purchaseCompleted",
  "correlationId": "server-issued-one-time-id"
}
```

Also:

- Enable JavaScript only if checkout requires it.
- Allow the bridge only while the normalized checkout origin and expected path are active.
- Reject malformed JSON, unknown fields/actions, oversized payloads, duplicates, and replays.
- Invalidate callbacks after navigation, disposal, logout, or account switching.
- Never accept a URL, fragment, page title, custom scheme, or bridge message as proof of payment.
- Keep the existing entitlement unchanged if backend verification fails.

The checkout backend must remain the authority that confirms payment and grants premium.
